;; ===========================================================================
;; ROE_Supplementary.kif
;;
;; The machinery for representing ROE measures. Nothing here is a Newport term:
;; these are the relations and rules introduced to state what a measure covers,
;; what follows when competent authority places it in force, and how measures
;; stand to one another.
;;
;; Architecture: a measure does not carry attributes describing itself. It
;; points at a defined class of action, and the content of the rule lives in
;; that class definition, which is written in ROE_Series.kif. Subsumption
;; between measures is then derived from &%subclass rather than stipulated by
;; matching tokens.
;;
;; Class definitions in ROE_Series.kif are written in the sufficient direction
;; only, that is conditions imply membership. The necessary direction was
;; measured and removed: it pulls in the SUMO axioms behind &%prevents and
;; &%hinders, which carry &%holdsDuring, &%decreasesLikelihood and nested
;; existentials, and the search space fills before the relevant clauses are
;; selected.
;;
;; Placement rule between this file and ROE_Series.kif: a term that cuts across
;; series belongs here, a term used by a single series belongs with that series.
;; The exception is a cross-cutting term that a Handbook term in ROE_Master.kif
;; depends on: &%Interference is such a term, since &%Harassment is placed under
;; it, so it is declared there rather than here to keep the load order forward.
;; &%ComplianceCompulsion is here because several series
;; turn on it; &%MissionInterference and &%ForceMovementProtection are in
;; ROE_Series.kif because Series 20 and Series 21 respectively are the only
;; series that use them.
;;
;; Load after ROE_Master.kif and before ROE_Series.kif.
;; ===========================================================================

;; Naming. Relations specific to this domain carry an roe prefix, so that a
;; name like roeActionAuthorized cannot be mistaken for a general relation
;; when the files are read alongside the rest of SUMO. Two relations are
;; deliberately left unprefixed. &%furthers is the positive counterpart of the
;; SUMO relation &%hinders, which has no positive form, and it is stated in
;; terms general enough to hold outside this domain; prefixing it would fix as
;; domain-specific something that is not. &%unsafeProximity belongs to the
;; COLREGs scenario pipeline rather than to ROE.

;; ===========================================================================
;; 87. ROE measure infrastructure
;; ===========================================================================

(subclass ROESeries Policy)
(documentation ROESeries EnglishWrittenLanguage "A numbered group of related ROE measures in the compendium at Annex B of the Newport ROE Handbook. Each series addresses a single subject matter and states a Purpose. &%Collection was considered and rejected as a parent: in SUMO a Collection is Physical and the first argument of &%member must be Physical, whereas measures are Propositions. &%Set would admit Proposition members but is extensional, and a series is more than the measures it contains, since it also carries a purpose and a default posture. A series is a &%Policy: a proposition set out by an authority to govern conduct, which SUMO gives an owner through &%policyOwner.")
(termFormat EnglishWrittenLanguage ROESeries "ROE series")

(subclass ROEMeasure Policy)
(documentation ROEMeasure EnglishWrittenLanguage "A single lettered rule within an &%ROESeries, for example rule 21 C. A measure is a catalog entry and binds no one on its own. It acquires normative effect only through an &%ROEActivation, by which competent authority places it in force for a particular operation and supplies any value its (SPECIFY ...) placeholder requires.")
(termFormat EnglishWrittenLanguage ROEMeasure "ROE measure")

(instance roeMeasureInSeries BinaryPredicate)
(domain roeMeasureInSeries 1 ROEMeasure)
(domain roeMeasureInSeries 2 ROESeries)
(documentation roeMeasureInSeries EnglishWrittenLanguage "(roeMeasureInSeries ?M ?S) means that the ROE measure ?M is a rule within the series ?S.")
(termFormat EnglishWrittenLanguage roeMeasureInSeries "measure in series")
(format EnglishWrittenLanguage roeMeasureInSeries "%1 is %n a measure in %2")

(instance roeMeasureAuthorizes BinaryPredicate)
(domain roeMeasureAuthorizes 1 ROEMeasure)
(domainSubclass roeMeasureAuthorizes 2 Process)
(documentation roeMeasureAuthorizes EnglishWrittenLanguage "(roeMeasureAuthorizes ?M ?CLASS) means that the ROE measure ?M authorizes actions of class ?CLASS. The scope of the measure is carried by the definition of ?CLASS. The Newport phrase 'up to and including deadly force' therefore needs no ceiling relation: a measure naming the wider class already authorizes every instance of its non-deadly subclass.")
(termFormat EnglishWrittenLanguage roeMeasureAuthorizes "measure authorizes")
(format EnglishWrittenLanguage roeMeasureAuthorizes "%1 %n authorizes actions of class %2")

(instance roeMeasureProhibits BinaryPredicate)
(domain roeMeasureProhibits 1 ROEMeasure)
(domainSubclass roeMeasureProhibits 2 Process)
(documentation roeMeasureProhibits EnglishWrittenLanguage "(roeMeasureProhibits ?M ?CLASS) means that the ROE measure ?M prohibits actions of class ?CLASS. A prohibition withholds authority over the whole class and so admits no ceiling.")
(termFormat EnglishWrittenLanguage roeMeasureProhibits "measure prohibits")
(format EnglishWrittenLanguage roeMeasureProhibits "%1 %n prohibits actions of class %2")

(instance roeMeasureRequiresParameter BinaryPredicate)
(domain roeMeasureRequiresParameter 1 ROEMeasure)
(domainSubclass roeMeasureRequiresParameter 2 Entity)
(documentation roeMeasureRequiresParameter EnglishWrittenLanguage "(roeMeasureRequiresParameter ?M ?CLASS) means the measure ?M carries a (SPECIFY ...) placeholder to be filled by an instance of ?CLASS. A measure carrying such a placeholder is a template rather than a complete norm, and the authorization rules of item 89 will not fire for it unless the corresponding &%roeActivationParameter is supplied.")
(termFormat EnglishWrittenLanguage roeMeasureRequiresParameter "measure requires parameter")
(format EnglishWrittenLanguage roeMeasureRequiresParameter "%1 %n requires a parameter of class %2")

;; The class named here is not checked by the authorization rules. Whether the
;; value supplied is of the right kind is a question about how the ROE were
;; issued, not about whether an action falls within them, so it belongs to
;; scenario well-formedness. Carrying it as a conjunct was also expensive: the
;; parameter class of rule 23 B is Object, which has 5,736 subclasses, and when
;; the conjunct failed to close, one query ran to 38,819 given-clause steps
;; without terminating. With the value correctly typed the same query proves
;; immediately, but a rule should not depend on a scenario being well formed
;; for its cost to stay bounded.

;; Complete and template measures are disjoint, but that is left unstated. It
;; was tried both ways. As (partition ROEMeasure ...) it was never selected and
;; changed nothing. As twenty-four explicit negative instance facts it was
;; selected constantly: a negative unit clause resolves against every positive
;; instance literal in the knowledge base, and in a saturating prover that is
;; expensive. Neither form is needed. Where a rule requires
;; (instance ?M CompleteROEMeasure) of a measure asserted only as a template,
;; the conjunct finds no ground fact to resolve against and the branch dies at
;; once. Absence of a ground fact is the cheap case; refuting a derived claim
;; is the expensive one, and the distinction is easy to confuse.

(subclass CompleteROEMeasure ROEMeasure)
(documentation CompleteROEMeasure EnglishWrittenLanguage "A measure that carries no (SPECIFY ...) placeholder and is therefore a complete norm as it stands in the compendium, for example rule 23 A. The distinction is drawn as a subclass rather than by a one-argument relation because SUMO has no idiom for such a relation: it provides Binary, Ternary, Quaternary and Quintary Predicate but nothing unary, and the two relations in the knowledge base that take only a first argument are declared VariableArityRelation. The distinction is also asserted positively rather than derived as the absence of a placeholder. The negative form (not (exists (?PC) (roeMeasureRequiresParameter ?M ?PC))) draws a Sigma warning for an existential in the antecedent, and simply dropping the existential is unsound: a free variable there is universally quantified over the whole rule, so the rule would fire on any class the measure does not happen to require, and the placeholder check would be bypassed.")
(termFormat EnglishWrittenLanguage CompleteROEMeasure "complete ROE measure")

(subclass TemplateROEMeasure ROEMeasure)
(documentation TemplateROEMeasure EnglishWrittenLanguage "A measure that carries a (SPECIFY ...) placeholder and is therefore a template rather than a complete norm, for example rule 21 E. It has effect only when an &%ROEActivation supplies a value through &%roeActivationParameter.")
(termFormat EnglishWrittenLanguage TemplateROEMeasure "template ROE measure")




;; Membership of both subclasses is asserted per measure rather than derived.
;; Deriving the template case from (roeMeasureRequiresParameter ?M ?PC) leaves ?PC
;; occurring in a single literal, which Sigma reports as a variable used only
;; once, and no reformulation avoids that: the rule turns on whether a
;; placeholder exists, not on which class fills it. The wrong-typed parameter
;; case is caught instead by Rule 4 of item 89, whose antecedent requires the
;; supplied value to be an instance of the required class.

(instance roeMeasureSubsumes BinaryPredicate)
(domain roeMeasureSubsumes 1 ROEMeasure)
(domain roeMeasureSubsumes 2 ROEMeasure)
(documentation roeMeasureSubsumes EnglishWrittenLanguage "(roeMeasureSubsumes ?M1 ?M2) means every action authorized by ?M2 is also authorized by ?M1. Transitivity and irreflexivity are not declared: the SUMO transitivity axiom carries a variable in predicate position, and expanding it enlarges the search space out of proportion to the number of classes involved here.")
(termFormat EnglishWrittenLanguage roeMeasureSubsumes "measure subsumes")
(format EnglishWrittenLanguage roeMeasureSubsumes "%1 %n subsumes %2")

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Activation
;;
;; An earlier version made roeInForce primitive and claimed in its
;; documentation that the operation argument bound any (SPECIFY ...)
;; placeholder. That claim was false: an operation cannot supply the person of
;; rule 21 E, the vessel of rule 22 B, or the instruction of rule 24 E. The act
;; of placing a measure in force is now a first-class individual carrying the
;; measure, the operation and the supplied value, and roeInForce is derived
;; from it.
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

;; Note on a measure kind distinction that was tried and removed. Permissive
;; and prohibitive measures were once separated into two subclasses, and the
;; action rules carried a conjunct naming the kind, in order to anchor the
;; disproof of roeActionProhibited: without it the prover must establish that a
;; permissive measure in force prohibits nothing. That turned out to need no
;; anchoring at all. Where a conjunct has no matching ground fact the branch
;; simply finds no resolution partner and dies, which is the cheap case; the
;; expensive case is a conjunct that has to be refuted through derived
;; machinery. Adding the distinction raised one disproof from 42 given-clause
;; steps to 39,153 and left another unterminated, so the cost was created by
;; the remedy rather than by the problem.

(subclass ROEActivation Proposition)
(documentation ROEActivation EnglishWrittenLanguage "An individual act of placing an &%ROEMeasure in force for a particular operation. It carries the measure through &%roeActivationMeasure, the operation through &%roeActivationOperation, and the value supplied for a (SPECIFY ...) placeholder through &%roeActivationParameter. Reifying the activation is what allows a parameterized measure to constrain particular actions rather than a whole class.")
(termFormat EnglishWrittenLanguage ROEActivation "ROE activation")

(instance roeActivationMeasure BinaryPredicate)
(domain roeActivationMeasure 1 ROEActivation)
(domain roeActivationMeasure 2 ROEMeasure)
(documentation roeActivationMeasure EnglishWrittenLanguage "(roeActivationMeasure ?ACTV ?M) means that the activation ?ACTV places the measure ?M in force.")
(termFormat EnglishWrittenLanguage roeActivationMeasure "activation measure")
(format EnglishWrittenLanguage roeActivationMeasure "%1 %n places %2 in force")

(instance roeActivationOperation BinaryPredicate)
(domain roeActivationOperation 1 ROEActivation)
(domain roeActivationOperation 2 MilitaryOperation)
(documentation roeActivationOperation EnglishWrittenLanguage "(roeActivationOperation ?ACTV ?OP) means that the activation ?ACTV places its measure in force for the operation ?OP.")
(termFormat EnglishWrittenLanguage roeActivationOperation "activation operation")
(format EnglishWrittenLanguage roeActivationOperation "%1 %n places its measure in force for %2")

(instance roeActivationParameter BinaryPredicate)
(domain roeActivationParameter 1 ROEActivation)
(domain roeActivationParameter 2 Entity)
(documentation roeActivationParameter EnglishWrittenLanguage "(roeActivationParameter ?ACTV ?X) means ?X is the entity supplied for the (SPECIFY ...) placeholder of the measure activated by ?ACTV. An action falls within a parameterized measure only if ?X is involved in it, in the sense of &%involvedInEvent.")
(termFormat EnglishWrittenLanguage roeActivationParameter "activation parameter")
(format EnglishWrittenLanguage roeActivationParameter "%1 %n supplies %2 as its parameter")

(instance roeInForce BinaryPredicate)
(domain roeInForce 1 ROEMeasure)
(domain roeInForce 2 MilitaryOperation)
(documentation roeInForce EnglishWrittenLanguage "(roeInForce ?M ?OP) means that the ROE measure ?M has been placed in force by competent authority for the operation ?OP. It is derived from an &%ROEActivation and says nothing about which value was supplied for a (SPECIFY ...) placeholder; that is carried by &%roeActivationParameter. A dedicated predicate is used rather than &%confersNorm, &%confersRight or &%permits because each of those takes a reified &%Formula as one of its arguments, and Formula-valued positions are where the search cost of the authorization rules concentrates.")
(termFormat EnglishWrittenLanguage roeInForce "ROE in force")
(format EnglishWrittenLanguage roeInForce "%1 is %n in force for %2")

(=>
  (and
    (roeActivationMeasure ?ACTV ?M)
    (roeActivationOperation ?ACTV ?OP))
  (roeInForce ?M ?OP))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Outcome predicates
;;
;; Only roeActionPermissible is a verdict for the operation. It requires three
;; gates: authority for the purpose of the act, from an AuthoritySeries;
;; clearance of prohibitions, the asserted closure roeNoProhibitionReaches; and
;; clearance of the means, the asserted closure roeMeansCleared. The verdict is
;; therefore conditional: under the measures recorded in force and the closure
;; assumptions asserted, the act is derived permissible. It is accepted only
;; for a scenario that passes the closure and measure-conflict checks, and the
;; failure to prove a conflict is not itself proof that none exists.
;;
;; The others are findings at the gate of a single measure or series:
;; roeActionAuthorized, roeActionProhibited, roeActionUnauthorized,
;; roeMeansAuthorized, roeActionHasDefaultPermission and roeDefaultOverlap. Findings from different series may hold of the same
;; action at once without inconsistency; an act that both advances the mission
;; and protects the movement of the Force can be unauthorized under Series 20
;; and authorized under rule 21 C.
;;
;; Among authority series, findings combine asymmetrically, and this is a
;; normative choice rather than an accident of the rules. Authority from any
;; one measure in force suffices,
;; so an unauthorized finding from one series does not defeat an authorization
;; from another. A prohibition from any one measure in force reaches the act,
;; so permissibility requires that none does. Each series regulates force for
;; its own purpose, and an act carried out for a purpose that a series in force
;; authorizes is authorized whatever other purposes it also serves. Between
;; purpose and means the combination is conjunctive instead: the Handbook
;; allows any lawful weapon for mission accomplishment unless another rule
;; limits it, so the means series limit an authority they do not grant.
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(instance roeActionAuthorized BinaryPredicate)
(domain roeActionAuthorized 1 Process)
(domain roeActionAuthorized 2 MilitaryOperation)
(documentation roeActionAuthorized EnglishWrittenLanguage "(roeActionAuthorized ?ACT ?OP) means the action ?ACT falls within the authority granted by some measure in force for the operation ?OP. It does not mean the action may be taken: a narrower measure may prohibit it, and necessity and proportionality are separate gates. The net result under ROE is &%roeActionPermissible.")
(termFormat EnglishWrittenLanguage roeActionAuthorized "action authorized")
(format EnglishWrittenLanguage roeActionAuthorized "%1 is %n authorized for %2")

(instance roeActionProhibited BinaryPredicate)
(domain roeActionProhibited 1 Process)
(domain roeActionProhibited 2 MilitaryOperation)
(documentation roeActionProhibited EnglishWrittenLanguage "(roeActionProhibited ?ACT ?OP) means the action ?ACT falls within a class prohibited by some measure in force for the operation ?OP. Authorization and prohibition are derived independently, so both may hold of one action. That is not by itself an error: Newport series are drafted so that a narrow prohibition may be layered on a broad permission, as rule 23 B is on rule 23 E.")
(termFormat EnglishWrittenLanguage roeActionProhibited "action prohibited")
(format EnglishWrittenLanguage roeActionProhibited "%1 is %n prohibited for %2")

(instance roeActionPermissible BinaryPredicate)
(domain roeActionPermissible 1 Process)
(domain roeActionPermissible 2 MilitaryOperation)
(documentation roeActionPermissible EnglishWrittenLanguage "(roeActionPermissible ?ACT ?OP) means that ?ACT is authorized for its purpose by a measure of an &%AuthoritySeries in force for ?OP, that no prohibition in force reaches it, and that every applicable means series has cleared its means. It is the only verdict of the authorization layer, and it is conditional: two of its conditions, &%roeNoProhibitionReaches and &%roeMeansCleared, are closure assumptions asserted for the scenario, so the verdict holds under those assumptions and the measures recorded in force, and is accepted only for a scenario that passes the closure and measure-conflict checks. It does not take account of an unauthorized finding from another authority series, by design: authority from one such series suffices. The absence of this verdict is not a prohibition. Force in individual or unit self-defense with no measure of Series 10 or 11 in force receives no verdict at all, only membership of &%InherentRightConduct, and no verdict must not be read as forbidden. Permissibility under ROE is not lawfulness: an action must still satisfy LOAC, necessity and proportionality.")
(termFormat EnglishWrittenLanguage roeActionPermissible "action permissible")
(format EnglishWrittenLanguage roeActionPermissible "%1 is %n permissible under the ROE for %2")

(instance roeNoProhibitionReaches BinaryPredicate)
(domain roeNoProhibitionReaches 1 Process)
(domain roeNoProhibitionReaches 2 MilitaryOperation)
(documentation roeNoProhibitionReaches EnglishWrittenLanguage "(roeNoProhibitionReaches ?ACT ?OP) states that no prohibitive measure in force for ?OP reaches ?ACT. It is asserted per scenario rather than derived. Writing the permissibility rule with (not (roeActionProhibited ?ACT ?OP)) in the antecedent instead makes the rule unable to fire at all: in first-order logic the failure to derive a prohibition does not license its negation, so an action that no prohibition reaches could never be shown permissible. This is the same closure discipline used for the restrictive default. The closure is stated over the measures in force rather than over the state of the world, and that asymmetry is deliberate. An ROE implementation message is an authoritative and complete statement of which measures a commander holds, so reading it as complete is not a guess. What an adversary is doing is known only as far as it has been observed, and no such assumption is made there.")
(termFormat EnglishWrittenLanguage roeNoProhibitionReaches "no prohibition reaches")
(format EnglishWrittenLanguage roeNoProhibitionReaches "no prohibition in force for %2 %n reaches %1")

(instance roeMeansAuthorized BinaryPredicate)
(domain roeMeansAuthorized 1 Process)
(domain roeMeansAuthorized 2 MilitaryOperation)
(documentation roeMeansAuthorized EnglishWrittenLanguage "(roeMeansAuthorized ?ACT ?OP) means that a measure of a &%MeansSeries in force for ?OP authorizes the means ?ACT employs, for example rule 84 D for the use of an autonomous weapon system. It is a gate finding. It is not authority for the act, which comes only from an &%AuthoritySeries, and it does not by itself clear the means gate, since another means series may also apply to the act; that is carried by the asserted closure &%roeMeansCleared.")
(termFormat EnglishWrittenLanguage roeMeansAuthorized "means authorized")
(format EnglishWrittenLanguage roeMeansAuthorized "the means of %1 is %n authorized for %2")

(instance roeMeansCleared BinaryPredicate)
(domain roeMeansCleared 1 Process)
(domain roeMeansCleared 2 MilitaryOperation)
(documentation roeMeansCleared EnglishWrittenLanguage "(roeMeansCleared ?ACT ?OP) states that every &%MeansSeries applicable to ?ACT has cleared the means ?ACT employs for ?OP, either by a measure in force authorizing it or by the permissive default of the series; where no means series applies, it holds vacuously. In the ordinary case it is derived from a narrower asserted closure about which means series apply: &%roeNoMeansSeriesReaches when none does, or &%roeOnlyMeansSeries when exactly one does, combined with a measure of that series in force authorizing the means or with the permissive default of that series, which then appears in the proof. Where two or more means series apply to the same act it is asserted directly. It is an explicit closure assumption subject to consistency checks, not a verified fact: the requirement that all applicable series have cleared the means is universal, and cannot be derived without negation. The checks in &%roeClosureConflict catch a means series whose subject the act falls in but which the closure leaves out, and an asserted clearance contradicted by a restrictive means series with no measure in force. They do not catch a series in force through a measure that does not reach ?ACT, for example rule 84 C for objectives other than the one ?ACT is directed at, which cannot be detected positively. Nor do they see Series 85, whose subject is fixed by the activation parameter and has no subject class, so a use of a weapon system specified under 85 is not checked at all. The preparation of the scenario must check both.")
(termFormat EnglishWrittenLanguage roeMeansCleared "means cleared")
(format EnglishWrittenLanguage roeMeansCleared "the means of %1 is %n cleared for %2")

(instance roeNoMeansSeriesReaches BinaryPredicate)
(domain roeNoMeansSeriesReaches 1 Process)
(domain roeNoMeansSeriesReaches 2 MilitaryOperation)
(documentation roeNoMeansSeriesReaches EnglishWrittenLanguage "(roeNoMeansSeriesReaches ?ACT ?OP) states that no &%MeansSeries applies to ?ACT in ?OP, as for a gun or missile that is not specially regulated. It is asserted per scenario, and &%roeMeansCleared follows from it vacuously. An act falling in the subject class of any means series contradicts it, which &%roeClosureConflict flags.")
(termFormat EnglishWrittenLanguage roeNoMeansSeriesReaches "no means series reaches")
(format EnglishWrittenLanguage roeNoMeansSeriesReaches "no means series %n applies to %1 in %2")

(instance roeOnlyMeansSeries TernaryPredicate)
(domain roeOnlyMeansSeries 1 Process)
(domain roeOnlyMeansSeries 2 ROESeries)
(domain roeOnlyMeansSeries 3 MilitaryOperation)
(documentation roeOnlyMeansSeries EnglishWrittenLanguage "(roeOnlyMeansSeries ?ACT ?S ?OP) states that ?S is the only &%MeansSeries applying to ?ACT in ?OP. It is asserted per scenario. Under it, &%roeMeansCleared is derived from a measure of ?S in force authorizing the means of ?ACT, or from the permissive default of ?S, and each derivation names ?S, so the measure or the default appears in the proof. An act falling in the subject class of a different means series contradicts it, which &%roeClosureConflict flags; the check relies on the means series being asserted distinct in ROE_Series.kif, since SUMO makes no unique names assumption. Each derivation also requires ?S to be a &%MeansSeries, stated in the rule rather than in the domain declaration. The domain of the second argument is deliberately left at &%ROESeries. Were it &%MeansSeries, SUMO's domain axiom would make any series named there a means series, so a closure wrongly naming an authority series such as Series 20 would place that series in two disjoint classes and make the knowledge base inconsistent, and Sigma would also reject or disable the check below. With the loose domain the error yields a flag instead. If ?S names an authority series, the clearance does not fire and &%roeClosureConflict flags the closure. If ?S names a means series whose subject class ?ACT is not in, the clearance does not fire and the error is left silent, since catching it would need negation; it is on the safe side, as it can withhold a verdict but cannot produce a wrong one.")
(termFormat EnglishWrittenLanguage roeOnlyMeansSeries "only means series")
(format EnglishWrittenLanguage roeOnlyMeansSeries "%2 is %n the only means series applying to %1 in %3")

;; ===========================================================================
;; 88. Issuing authority
;;
;; The authority whose ROE govern an operation. It scopes the permissive
;; default of a means series to operations conducted under ROE at all.
;;
;; The deontic layer that used to follow here, asserting the SUMO relations
;; &%permits and &%prohibits of the issuing authority for each measure in
;; force, has been moved to ROE_Deontic.kif, which is not loaded with the main
;; knowledge base. Law.kif makes permitting and prohibiting the same formula
;; inconsistent, so a scenario that put rules 84 A and 84 D in force together
;; made the whole knowledge base inconsistent, and an inconsistent knowledge
;; base proves every query, including those a test expects to fail. The flag
;; &%roeMeasureConflict could not make that visible. The deontic rules were not
;; on any inference path of the authorization layer, which runs on
;; &%roeActionAuthorized because &%permits and &%prohibits take a reified
;; &%Formula, where search cost concentrates.
;; ===========================================================================

(instance roeIssuingAuthority BinaryPredicate)
(domain roeIssuingAuthority 1 MilitaryOperation)
(domain roeIssuingAuthority 2 AutonomousAgent)
(documentation roeIssuingAuthority EnglishWrittenLanguage "(roeIssuingAuthority ?OP ?AUTH) means ?AUTH is the authority whose ROE govern the operation ?OP. In a multinational force different contributing States may impose different ROE on their own units, so the authority is carried explicitly rather than assumed.")
(termFormat EnglishWrittenLanguage roeIssuingAuthority "issuing authority")
(format EnglishWrittenLanguage roeIssuingAuthority "%2 is %n the issuing authority of the ROE for %1")

;; Rule 1: The authority behind an operation is the headquarters that issued
;; the ROE authorization to the unit conducting it.
(=>
  (and
    (instance ?AUTHMSG ROEAuthorization)
    (agent ?AUTHMSG ?HQ)
    (instance ?HQ HigherHeadquarters)
    (destination ?AUTHMSG ?UNIT)
    (instance ?OP MilitaryOperation)
    (agent ?OP ?UNIT))
  (roeIssuingAuthority ?OP ?HQ))

;; ===========================================================================
;; 89. Derived structure
;; ===========================================================================

;; Rule 1: A measure subsumes another when the class it authorizes includes
;; the class the other authorizes.
(=>
  (and
    (roeMeasureAuthorizes ?M1 ?C1)
    (roeMeasureAuthorizes ?M2 ?C2)
    (subclass ?C2 ?C1))
  (roeMeasureSubsumes ?M1 ?M2))

;; Rule 2: An authorization whose class falls within the class of a prohibition
;; in force for the same operation is flagged as a measure conflict, for
;; example rules 21 A and 21 B, or 84 A and 84 D. The rule is deliberately
;; asymmetric. A narrow prohibition combined with a broad authorization is not
;; flagged, because that combination is how ROE are layered in practice, for
;; example rule 23 B with rule 23 E; that case is resolved at the action level
;; by Rule 6. The flagged direction differs in kind. A broad prohibition with a
;; narrow authorization inside it, such as 23 A with 23 D, are alternative
;; entries of one matrix: 23 A says warning shots may not be used at all, and
;; 23 D says they may be used to compel compliance. Selecting both is not a
;; layered rule but a contradictory choice, since the narrower permission
;; cannot be exercised without breaching the broader prohibition. An earlier version concluded (not (roeInForce ?M2 ?OP)) here,
;; which made the knowledge base inconsistent whenever a scenario did put both
;; measures in force, and an inconsistent knowledge base proves every query.
(instance roeMeasureConflict BinaryPredicate)
(domain roeMeasureConflict 1 ROEMeasure)
(domain roeMeasureConflict 2 MilitaryOperation)
(documentation roeMeasureConflict EnglishWrittenLanguage "(roeMeasureConflict ?M ?OP) means that the authorizing measure ?M is in force for ?OP together with a prohibiting measure whose class contains the class ?M authorizes. It does not mean that ?M is invalid or out of force: both measures remain in force, and the flag records that ?M is party to a detected conflict. The prohibiting measure on the other side is shown by the derivation, not by the predicate, which is kept binary for search cost. It is a class-level check: it compares the classes the two measures name, not particular acts. Where either measure carries a placeholder, the values supplied are not compared, so two activations for different persons or objectives, for example rule 21 D for some persons and rule 21 F for others, are flagged although they may never reach the same act; such a flag marks a potential conflict to be checked, not an error. Where both measures are complete, a scenario in which this predicate is derived is in error, and results derived from it are not accepted. Classes that overlap without one containing the other are not flagged: an act in both receives &%roeActionAuthorized and &%roeActionProhibited, and Rule 6 then withholds the verdict, since it requires that no prohibition reaches the act.")
(termFormat EnglishWrittenLanguage roeMeasureConflict "measure conflict")
(format EnglishWrittenLanguage roeMeasureConflict "%1 is %n in a measure conflict in %2")

(=>
  (and
    (roeMeasureProhibits ?M1 ?C1)
    (roeMeasureAuthorizes ?M2 ?C2)
    (subclass ?C2 ?C1)
    (roeInForce ?M1 ?OP)
    (roeInForce ?M2 ?OP))
  (roeMeasureConflict ?M2 ?OP))
;; Rule 2b: The same check where both measures name the same class, as rules
;; 84 A and 84 D do. Rule 2 covers this case only through (subclass ?C ?C).
;; SUMO makes subclass reflexive, but in the TPTP translation that fact is
;; reached only through reflexiveOn and the ReflexiveRelation axiom, which the
;; prover did not find: test ROE39 returned no proof on 19 October, and
;; CounterSatisfiable on the core knowledge base. Stated directly, it proves.
(=>
  (and
    (roeMeasureProhibits ?M1 ?C)
    (roeMeasureAuthorizes ?M2 ?C)
    (roeInForce ?M1 ?OP)
    (roeInForce ?M2 ?OP))
  (roeMeasureConflict ?M2 ?OP))

;; Rule 3: An action is authorized when it is an instance of a class
;; authorized by a measure in force that carries no placeholder.
(=>
  (and
    (roeActivationMeasure ?ACTV ?M)
    (roeActivationOperation ?ACTV ?OP)
    (instance ?M CompleteROEMeasure)
    (roeMeasureInSeries ?M ?S)
    (instance ?S AuthoritySeries)
    (roeMeasureAuthorizes ?M ?C)
    (instance ?ACT ?C))
  (roeActionAuthorized ?ACT ?OP))

;; Rule 4: An action is authorized under a measure that carries a placeholder
;; only when the supplied value is involved in the action. Without this
;; conjunct rule 21 E would authorize force protecting the movement of anyone
;; at all, rather than of the persons actually specified.
(=>
  (and
    (roeActivationMeasure ?ACTV ?M)
    (roeActivationOperation ?ACTV ?OP)
    (roeActivationParameter ?ACTV ?X)
    (instance ?M TemplateROEMeasure)
    (roeMeasureInSeries ?M ?S)
    (instance ?S AuthoritySeries)
    (roeMeasureAuthorizes ?M ?C)
    (instance ?ACT ?C)
    (involvedInEvent ?ACT ?X))
  (roeActionAuthorized ?ACT ?OP))

;; Rules 3b and 4b: An authorizing measure of a means series does not authorize
;; the act. It records that the means the act employs has been authorized, a
;; finding that bears on the asserted closure roeMeansCleared but does not
;; establish it, since another means series may also apply to the act.
(=>
  (and
    (roeActivationMeasure ?ACTV ?M)
    (roeActivationOperation ?ACTV ?OP)
    (instance ?M CompleteROEMeasure)
    (roeMeasureInSeries ?M ?S)
    (instance ?S MeansSeries)
    (roeMeasureAuthorizes ?M ?C)
    (instance ?ACT ?C))
  (roeMeansAuthorized ?ACT ?OP))

(=>
  (and
    (roeActivationMeasure ?ACTV ?M)
    (roeActivationOperation ?ACTV ?OP)
    (roeActivationParameter ?ACTV ?X)
    (instance ?M TemplateROEMeasure)
    (roeMeasureInSeries ?M ?S)
    (instance ?S MeansSeries)
    (roeMeasureAuthorizes ?M ?C)
    (instance ?ACT ?C)
    (involvedInEvent ?ACT ?X))
  (roeMeansAuthorized ?ACT ?OP))

;; Rule 5: An action is prohibited when it is an instance of a class
;; prohibited by a measure in force that carries no placeholder.
(=>
  (and
    (roeActivationMeasure ?ACTV ?M)
    (roeActivationOperation ?ACTV ?OP)
    (instance ?M CompleteROEMeasure)
    (roeMeasureProhibits ?M ?C)
    (instance ?ACT ?C))
  (roeActionProhibited ?ACT ?OP))

;; Rule 5b: A prohibition that carries a placeholder reaches only actions in
;; which the supplied value is involved. Rule 23 B forbids warning shots in the
;; vicinity of specified elements, not in the vicinity of anything whatever.
(=>
  (and
    (roeActivationMeasure ?ACTV ?M)
    (roeActivationOperation ?ACTV ?OP)
    (roeActivationParameter ?ACTV ?X)
    (instance ?M TemplateROEMeasure)
    (roeMeasureProhibits ?M ?C)
    (instance ?ACT ?C)
    (involvedInEvent ?ACT ?X))
  (roeActionProhibited ?ACT ?OP))

;; Rules 6a to 6d: The means closure. Where no means series applies, the means
;; is cleared vacuously. Where exactly one applies, ?S, it is cleared by a
;; measure of ?S in force authorizing it (6b, 6c) or by the permissive default
;; of ?S (6d). Each rule names ?S in its own antecedent, so the clearance is
;; tied to that series in the rule itself and in the proof, and does not rest
;; on the check of Rule 8d. An earlier version used the series-blind finding
;; roeMeansAuthorized here; that was sound only because Rule 8d flags any act
;; also falling under a second means series.
(=>
  (roeNoMeansSeriesReaches ?ACT ?OP)
  (roeMeansCleared ?ACT ?OP))

(=>
  (and
    (roeOnlyMeansSeries ?ACT ?S ?OP)
    (instance ?S MeansSeries)
    (roeActivationMeasure ?ACTV ?M)
    (roeActivationOperation ?ACTV ?OP)
    (instance ?M CompleteROEMeasure)
    (roeMeasureInSeries ?M ?S)
    (roeMeasureAuthorizes ?M ?C)
    (instance ?ACT ?C))
  (roeMeansCleared ?ACT ?OP))

(=>
  (and
    (roeOnlyMeansSeries ?ACT ?S ?OP)
    (instance ?S MeansSeries)
    (roeActivationMeasure ?ACTV ?M)
    (roeActivationOperation ?ACTV ?OP)
    (roeActivationParameter ?ACTV ?X)
    (instance ?M TemplateROEMeasure)
    (roeMeasureInSeries ?M ?S)
    (roeMeasureAuthorizes ?M ?C)
    (instance ?ACT ?C)
    (involvedInEvent ?ACT ?X))
  (roeMeansCleared ?ACT ?OP))

(=>
  (and
    (roeOnlyMeansSeries ?ACT ?S ?OP)
    (instance ?S MeansSeries)
    (instance ?S PermissiveDefaultSeries)
    (roeSeriesAddresses ?S ?C)
    (instance ?ACT ?C)
    (roeActionHasDefaultPermission ?ACT ?OP))
  (roeMeansCleared ?ACT ?OP))

;; Rule 6: An action authorized for its purpose, which no measure in force
;; prohibits and whose means every applicable means series has cleared, is
;; permissible. Where an authorization and a prohibition both reach the
;; action the prohibition governs, since the more specific rule defeats the
;; more general. Two of the three conditions are asserted closures.
(=>
  (and
    (roeActionAuthorized ?ACT ?OP)
    (roeNoProhibitionReaches ?ACT ?OP)
    (roeMeansCleared ?ACT ?OP))
  (roeActionPermissible ?ACT ?OP))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; The restrictive default
;;
;; The Newport Handbook takes a restrictive approach: an action is not
;; authorized unless a measure authorizes it. Represented as the failure to
;; derive roeActionAuthorized, that default yields a saturation result rather than
;; a proof, and so carries no inference chain for what is in fact the ordinary
;; case. It is stated positively instead, from an asserted closure fact.
;;
;; Not every series follows the restrictive default. Some state in their notes
;; that the means they regulate is permitted unless restricted (Series 83, and
;; 70 and 133 when encoded). Others regulate a right that does not derive from
;; the ROE at all: inherent self-defense (Series 10 and 11) and freedom of
;; navigation under UNCLOS (Series 58). Each series is labelled accordingly,
;; and the default rules below apply only to series carrying the matching
;; label. The source of a right and the default policy are separate
;; dimensions, so a pre-existing right series may still be restrictive, as
;; Series 13 and 14 are. Only the inherent right series is disjoint from the
;; restrictive label, so the restrictive default can never reach individual or
;; unit self-defense through a labelling slip.
;;
;; A permissive default is not a source of authority. It says that the means
;; needs no separate authorization, not that the act is authorized: every use
;; of force has a purpose, and authority for the purpose comes from a purpose
;; series (20 to 24) or from an inherent right. An earlier version derived
;; permissibility from the default permission directly, and a use of a
;; semi-autonomous weapon to accomplish the mission became permissible while
;; Series 20 was in force in no part, so the default of a means series was
;; breaking through the restriction of a purpose series. That rule was removed.
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(instance roeSeriesAddresses BinaryPredicate)
(domain roeSeriesAddresses 1 ROESeries)
(domainSubclass roeSeriesAddresses 2 Process)
(documentation roeSeriesAddresses EnglishWrittenLanguage "(roeSeriesAddresses ?S ?CLASS) means that some measure of series ?S bears on actions of class ?CLASS, so that ?S is the series to consult about them. The subject classes of each series are asserted directly in ROE_Series.kif, as a property of the series, and the two rules below derive the same facts from the measures, which keeps them in step. The facts are asserted because the derived form was measured to be too costly: on 13 October every positive test whose restrictive default ran through a derived subject class returned Unknown or Disproved, while the one whose class was asserted (Series 84) proved.")
(termFormat EnglishWrittenLanguage roeSeriesAddresses "series addresses")
(format EnglishWrittenLanguage roeSeriesAddresses "%1 %n addresses actions of class %2")

(=>
  (and
    (roeMeasureInSeries ?M ?S)
    (roeMeasureAuthorizes ?M ?C))
  (roeSeriesAddresses ?S ?C))

(=>
  (and
    (roeMeasureInSeries ?M ?S)
    (roeMeasureProhibits ?M ?C))
  (roeSeriesAddresses ?S ?C))

;; Purpose and means

(subclass AuthoritySeries ROESeries)
(documentation AuthoritySeries EnglishWrittenLanguage "An &%ROESeries regulating force or conduct by its purpose, whose authorizing measures grant authority for the act: the self-defense series 10 to 14, the mission and protection series 20 to 24, Series 58, and Series 60 and 61. Series 10 and 11 are placed here although the right they regulate does not derive from the ROE. Their measures do not create the right; they state how the ROE express its exercise, for example the limit to non-deadly force of rule 10 B, and an act within such a measure is authorized in the same sense as under any other. The right itself is carried separately by &%InherentRightConduct, which holds whether or not a measure of these series is in force.")
(termFormat EnglishWrittenLanguage AuthoritySeries "authority series")

(subclass MeansSeries ROESeries)
(documentation MeansSeries EnglishWrittenLanguage "An &%ROESeries regulating the means by which force is used, Series 83, 84 and 85. Its authorizing measures do not authorize the act, whose authority comes from an &%AuthoritySeries; they clear the means, recorded by &%roeMeansAuthorized. The Handbook states the principle in its notes to the weapons series: commanders may use any lawful weapon available for mission accomplishment, unless and to the extent of any additional limitation imposed by another rule. A means series therefore limits an authority it does not grant, and purpose and means are both required.")
(termFormat EnglishWrittenLanguage MeansSeries "means series")

(disjoint AuthoritySeries MeansSeries)

;; Default types of a series

(subclass RestrictiveDefaultSeries ROESeries)
(documentation RestrictiveDefaultSeries EnglishWrittenLanguage "An &%ROESeries whose subject matter is unauthorized unless a measure of the series authorizes it. This is the general position of the Newport Handbook (paragraph 22): ROE are a statement of what is permitted, so where no measure of the series has been placed in force for an operation, no authority for the actions it addresses exists.")
(termFormat EnglishWrittenLanguage RestrictiveDefaultSeries "restrictive default series")

(subclass PermissiveDefaultSeries ROESeries)
(documentation PermissiveDefaultSeries EnglishWrittenLanguage "An &%ROESeries whose subject matter is permitted unless a measure of the series prohibits or limits it. The Handbook identifies such series individually in their notes, for example Series 83, which assumes that use of a semi-autonomous weapon system is permitted unless prohibited or limited by a rule. The default concerns the means only. It does not authorize the act, whose authority must still come from the series governing its purpose or from an inherent right.")
(termFormat EnglishWrittenLanguage PermissiveDefaultSeries "permissive default series")

(disjoint RestrictiveDefaultSeries PermissiveDefaultSeries)

(subclass PreExistingRightSeries ROESeries)
(documentation PreExistingRightSeries EnglishWrittenLanguage "An &%ROESeries regulating the exercise of a right that exists independently of the ROE, in law outside them. Freedom of navigation and overflight under UNCLOS (Series 58) is the case encoded so far. The label concerns the source of the right only and says nothing about the default policy: a right may pre-exist the ROE while its exercise by a unit still requires authority, so this class is not disjoint from &%RestrictiveDefaultSeries.")
(termFormat EnglishWrittenLanguage PreExistingRightSeries "pre-existing right series")

(subclass InherentRightSeries PreExistingRightSeries)
(documentation InherentRightSeries EnglishWrittenLanguage "An &%PreExistingRightSeries regulating the inherent right of individual and unit self-defense (Series 10 and 11). The Handbook states that nothing in ROE limits that right, and these series accordingly contain no measure prohibiting it. That is why this class, unlike its superclass, is disjoint from &%RestrictiveDefaultSeries: the absence of a measure of these series never leaves self-defense unauthorized. National and collective self-defense (Series 13 and 14) also rest on an inherent right, that of the State under Article 51 of the UN Charter, but the exercise of that right by a unit requires authority, so those series are restrictive at the level of the ROE and are not placed here.")
(termFormat EnglishWrittenLanguage InherentRightSeries "inherent right series")

(disjoint InherentRightSeries RestrictiveDefaultSeries)

(subclass InherentRightConduct UseOfForce)
(documentation InherentRightConduct EnglishWrittenLanguage "A process belonging to a category of conduct whose right is the inherent right of individual or unit self-defense. Membership is a statement about the category, not about the particular act: it does not establish that the act is a lawful exercise of the right, for which necessity, proportionality and the imminence of the threat must still be assessed, and none of these is represented here. Its role is to keep the absence of an ROE measure from being read as the absence of the right; see &%roeDefaultOverlap. Drawn as a class rather than a one-argument relation because SUMO has no unary predicate idiom, as noted for &%CompleteROEMeasure.")
(termFormat EnglishWrittenLanguage InherentRightConduct "inherent right conduct")

(=>
  (and
    (instance ?S InherentRightSeries)
    (roeSeriesAddresses ?S ?C)
    (instance ?ACT ?C))
  (instance ?ACT InherentRightConduct))

(instance roeActionHasDefaultPermission BinaryPredicate)
(domain roeActionHasDefaultPermission 1 Process)
(domain roeActionHasDefaultPermission 2 MilitaryOperation)
(documentation roeActionHasDefaultPermission EnglishWrittenLanguage "(roeActionHasDefaultPermission ?ACT ?OP) means that ?ACT falls under a &%PermissiveDefaultSeries and ?OP is conducted under ROE issued by some authority, so that this series clears the means ?ACT employs without a measure being placed in force. It is a gate finding. It is not a source of authority, and it does not establish &%roeMeansCleared, since another means series may also apply to the act. It bears on that closure assumption, and can be queried alongside the verdict to show how the means gate was met. A measure in force prohibiting the means still reaches the act.")
(termFormat EnglishWrittenLanguage roeActionHasDefaultPermission "action has default permission")
(format EnglishWrittenLanguage roeActionHasDefaultPermission "%1 %n has default permission for %2")

;; The default permission is tied to the series and to the operation being
;; under ROE at all, not to whether some measure of the series is in force, so
;; a measure restricting part of the subject matter does not remove the
;; default for the remainder. The operation is scoped by roeIssuingAuthority,
;; which follows from the ROE authorization message rather than from any
;; particular activation.
;; ?AUTH is universally quantified in the antecedent, which is equivalent to an
;; existential there. An earlier version wrote the existential explicitly,
;; which Sigma rejects; the type conjunct on ?AUTH keeps the variable from
;; occurring only once.
(=>
  (and
    (instance ?S PermissiveDefaultSeries)
    (roeSeriesAddresses ?S ?C)
    (instance ?ACT ?C)
    (roeIssuingAuthority ?OP ?AUTH)
    (instance ?AUTH AutonomousAgent))
  (roeActionHasDefaultPermission ?ACT ?OP))

(instance roeSeriesNotAddressed BinaryPredicate)
(domain roeSeriesNotAddressed 1 ROESeries)
(domain roeSeriesNotAddressed 2 MilitaryOperation)
(documentation roeSeriesNotAddressed EnglishWrittenLanguage "(roeSeriesNotAddressed ?S ?OP) states that no measure of series ?S has been placed in force for operation ?OP. It is asserted per scenario. Deriving it from the absence of an activation would require treating the record of activations as complete. That assumption is warranted here, since an ROE implementation message states in full which measures are held, but it is not warranted about the state of the world and is not made there. Because the fact is asserted rather than derived, it can fall out of step with the activations recorded alongside it; &%roeClosureConflict is the check for that.")
(termFormat EnglishWrittenLanguage roeSeriesNotAddressed "series not addressed")
(format EnglishWrittenLanguage roeSeriesNotAddressed "%1 is %n not addressed for %2")

(instance roeActionUnauthorized BinaryPredicate)
(domain roeActionUnauthorized 1 Process)
(domain roeActionUnauthorized 2 MilitaryOperation)
(documentation roeActionUnauthorized EnglishWrittenLanguage "(roeActionUnauthorized ?ACT ?OP) indicates that at least one applicable &%RestrictiveDefaultSeries provides no authorization for ?ACT in ?OP, because no measure of that series has been placed in force. It holds only under the asserted closure &%roeSeriesNotAddressed. It is a finding at the gate of one series and not a verdict: it does not establish that ?ACT is unauthorized under the ROE as a whole, and it does not negate an authorization derived from another series. Which series produced it is shown by the derivation, not by the predicate. Distinct from &%roeActionProhibited, which requires a measure forbidding the action: an unauthorized action is one no one was given, a prohibited action is one someone was refused.")
(termFormat EnglishWrittenLanguage roeActionUnauthorized "action unauthorized")
(format EnglishWrittenLanguage roeActionUnauthorized "%1 is %n unauthorized for %2")

(=>
  (and
    (instance ?S RestrictiveDefaultSeries)
    (roeSeriesNotAddressed ?S ?OP)
    (roeSeriesAddresses ?S ?C)
    (instance ?ACT ?C))
  (roeActionUnauthorized ?ACT ?OP))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Closure conflict
;;
;; The two closure predicates above are asserted rather than derived, so a
;; scenario can state one of them while also recording facts that contradict
;; it: a prohibition that does reach the action, or an activation of a measure
;; from the series said not to be addressed. Nothing in the logic prevents
;; this, and the resulting conclusions would be wrong rather than unprovable.
;;
;; The conflict is flagged with a derived predicate rather than by deriving a
;; negation of the asserted fact. Deriving the negation would make the
;; knowledge base inconsistent, and an inconsistent knowledge base returns
;; Theorem for every query, so a data entry error would silently satisfy every
;; test expecting a positive answer. A flag leaves the knowledge base
;; consistent and can be queried directly, at the cost of having to be asked.
;; Scenario files should therefore carry (query (roeClosureConflict ...)) with
;; (answer no) alongside their substantive queries.
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(instance roeClosureConflict BinaryPredicate)
(domain roeClosureConflict 1 Entity)
(domain roeClosureConflict 2 MilitaryOperation)
(documentation roeClosureConflict EnglishWrittenLanguage "(roeClosureConflict ?X ?OP) means that a closure fact asserted for ?OP is contradicted by the activations recorded for ?OP. The first argument is the action or the series the closure fact was asserted about. Deriving this predicate indicates an error in the scenario, not a conflict of norms. The conflict is detected, not repaired: conclusions already derived from the erroneous closure fact, such as &%roeActionPermissible, remain derivable. A conclusion of the authorization layer is therefore accepted only for a scenario in which neither a closure conflict nor a &%roeMeasureConflict has been derived. This is a condition on reading results, applied outside the logic; it is not a rule that treats the failure to prove a conflict as its negation.")
(termFormat EnglishWrittenLanguage roeClosureConflict "closure conflict")
(format EnglishWrittenLanguage roeClosureConflict "a closure fact about %1 is %n in conflict for %2")

;; Rule 7: An action said to be reached by no prohibition, which a measure in
;; force does prohibit.
(=>
  (and
    (roeNoProhibitionReaches ?ACT ?OP)
    (roeActionProhibited ?ACT ?OP))
  (roeClosureConflict ?ACT ?OP))

;; Rule 8: A series said not to be addressed, one of whose measures is in force.
(=>
  (and
    (roeSeriesNotAddressed ?S ?OP)
    (roeMeasureInSeries ?M ?S)
    (roeInForce ?M ?OP))
  (roeClosureConflict ?S ?OP))

;; Rule 8c: An action said to be reached by no means series, which falls in
;; the subject class of one.
(=>
  (and
    (roeNoMeansSeriesReaches ?ACT ?OP)
    (instance ?S MeansSeries)
    (roeSeriesAddresses ?S ?C)
    (instance ?ACT ?C))
  (roeClosureConflict ?ACT ?OP))

;; Rule 8d: An action said to fall under one means series only, which falls in
;; the subject class of another. The disequality is a classical literal, not
;; negation as failure; it is satisfied by the ground facts in ROE_Series.kif
;; asserting the means series distinct.
(=>
  (and
    (roeOnlyMeansSeries ?ACT ?S ?OP)
    (instance ?S2 MeansSeries)
    (roeSeriesAddresses ?S2 ?C)
    (instance ?ACT ?C)
    (not
      (equal ?S ?S2)))
  (roeClosureConflict ?ACT ?OP))

;; Rule 8e: A means closure naming a series that is not a means series. Without
;; the (instance ?S MeansSeries) conjunct of Rules 6b to 6d, a closure naming a
;; purpose series such as Series 20 would have let a purpose measure clear the
;; means gate; with it, the clearance does not fire, and this rule makes the
;; error visible rather than silent. AuthoritySeries and MeansSeries are
;; disjoint, so the check rests on a positive label.
(=>
  (and
    (roeOnlyMeansSeries ?ACT ?S ?OP)
    (instance ?S AuthoritySeries))
  (roeClosureConflict ?ACT ?OP))

;; Rule 8b: An action whose means is said to be cleared, which falls under a
;; restrictive means series none of whose measures is in force. The absence of
;; measures is itself the asserted closure roeSeriesNotAddressed, so the check
;; rests on no negation. A series in force through a measure that does not
;; reach the action is not caught here; see roeMeansCleared.
(=>
  (and
    (roeMeansCleared ?ACT ?OP)
    (instance ?S MeansSeries)
    (instance ?S RestrictiveDefaultSeries)
    (roeSeriesNotAddressed ?S ?OP)
    (roeSeriesAddresses ?S ?C)
    (instance ?ACT ?C))
  (roeClosureConflict ?ACT ?OP))

(instance roeDefaultOverlap BinaryPredicate)
(domain roeDefaultOverlap 1 Process)
(domain roeDefaultOverlap 2 MilitaryOperation)
(documentation roeDefaultOverlap EnglishWrittenLanguage "(roeDefaultOverlap ?ACT ?OP) indicates that an act belonging to the category of inherent self-defense also receives an unauthorized finding from an applicable restrictive-default series, for example force in unit self-defense that also advances the mission when Series 20 is in force in no part. The overlap is not by itself a logical inconsistency, a scenario error, or a determination that the inherent right is invalid. Two gate findings hold of the same act: &%roeActionUnauthorized from the purpose series and membership of &%InherentRightConduct. The Handbook holds that nothing in ROE limits the inherent right of self-defense, so the restrictive default of the purpose series does not govern the act; the predicate makes that visible when the result is read. &%MissionAccomplishmentForce is deliberately not disjoint from self-defense, so the overlap cannot be ruled out by classification.")
(termFormat EnglishWrittenLanguage roeDefaultOverlap "default overlap")
(format EnglishWrittenLanguage roeDefaultOverlap "%1 %n falls under a restrictive default and an inherent right in %2")

;; Rule 9: An act left unauthorized by a restrictive default, which belongs to
;; the category of inherent self-defense.
(=>
  (and
    (roeActionUnauthorized ?ACT ?OP)
    (instance ?ACT InherentRightConduct))
  (roeDefaultOverlap ?ACT ?OP))

;; ===========================================================================
;; 90. Shared action qualifications
;; ===========================================================================

(instance furthers BinaryPredicate)
(domain furthers 1 Process)
(domain furthers 2 Process)
(documentation furthers EnglishWrittenLanguage "(furthers ?P1 ?P2) means that ?P1 is carried out in order to bring about or advance ?P2. SUMO provides &%hinders for the negative case but has no positive counterpart holding between two process individuals: &%hasPurpose is the nearest relation and takes a reified &%Formula as its second argument, which the authorization rules cannot carry. This relation is introduced to fill that gap. It is deliberately narrower than &%subProcess, which says only that one process is part of another and would classify every use of force occurring during an operation, including force used in self-defense, as force for mission accomplishment.")
(termFormat EnglishWrittenLanguage furthers "furthers")
(format EnglishWrittenLanguage furthers "%1 %n furthers %2")

(instance roeCompelsComplianceWith BinaryPredicate)
(domain roeCompelsComplianceWith 1 Process)
(domain roeCompelsComplianceWith 2 Order)
(documentation roeCompelsComplianceWith EnglishWrittenLanguage "(roeCompelsComplianceWith ?P ?ORD) means that ?P is carried out in order to induce the party addressed by ?ORD to comply with it. ?ORD is the order itself, a &%Sentence, which in SUMO is the result of an &%Ordering process. The relation takes the order rather than the act of ordering because the compendium phrase 'to compel compliance with (SPECIFY instructions)' refers to instructions already given, whose content is what compliance is measured against.")
(termFormat EnglishWrittenLanguage roeCompelsComplianceWith "compels compliance with")
(format EnglishWrittenLanguage roeCompelsComplianceWith "%1 %n compels compliance with %2")

(subclass ComplianceCompulsion MilitaryProcess)
(documentation ComplianceCompulsion EnglishWrittenLanguage "A process carried out to induce compliance with an order already issued. The Newport compendium uses the phrase 'to compel compliance with (SPECIFY instructions)' in several series, and this class carries that qualification once rather than repeating it in each. Membership turns on &%roeCompelsComplianceWith: an earlier version derived it from the process and the order merely sharing a target, which made any process directed at the addressee of any order a compliance compulsion.")
(termFormat EnglishWrittenLanguage ComplianceCompulsion "compliance compulsion")

(=>
  (and
    (instance ?P Process)
    (instance ?ORD Order)
    (roeCompelsComplianceWith ?P ?ORD))
  (instance ?P ComplianceCompulsion))

;; ===========================================================================
;; 91. Behavioural predicates
;; Predicates with no SUMO equivalent, used by the COLREGs scenario pipeline.
;; ===========================================================================

(instance unsafeProximity TernaryPredicate)
(domain unsafeProximity 1 Ship)
(domain unsafeProximity 2 Ship)
(domain unsafeProximity 3 LengthMeasure)
(documentation unsafeProximity EnglishWrittenLanguage
  "(&%unsafeProximity ?warship ?vessel ?distance) holds when
   ?warship closes the distance to ?vessel within ?distance,
   requiring the master of ?vessel to make repeated course or
   speed adjustments under COLREGs Rule 8 and Rule 16.")
(termFormat EnglishWrittenLanguage unsafeProximity "unsafe proximity")
(format EnglishWrittenLanguage unsafeProximity "%1 is %n within %3 of %2")
